Instead of combining strings immediately, this builds a small expression tree. An operator becomes a parent node, while the two popped items become its left and right children. A final traversal then prints the tree with parentheses.

For `xy-z+`, the tree keeps `x-y` together before adding `z`. This makes the operand order easy to see in the code.

```python
class Solution:
    def post_to_infix(self, post_exp: str) -> str:
        operators = set("+-*/^")
        stack = []

        for token in post_exp:
            if token.isspace():
                continue

            if token.isalnum():
                stack.append((token, None, None))
            elif token in operators:
                right = stack.pop()
                left = stack.pop()
                stack.append((token, left, right))

        if not stack:
            return ""

        result = []
        work = [(stack[-1], False)]

        while work:
            node, closing = work.pop()
            value, left, right = node

            if left is None:
                result.append(value)
                continue

            if closing:
                result.append(")")
            else:
                result.append("(")
                work.append((node, True))
                work.append((right, False))
                result.append(value)
                work.append((left, False))

        return "".join(result)
```

The dry run for `ab+c*` creates a `+` node for `a` and `b`, then makes that node the left child of `*` with `c` on the right.

Time complexity: `O(n)` for scanning and visiting the tree.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the tree and traversal stack.

A small operand stack is enough here. Each operand is pushed as it appears. When an operator is found, the top two expressions are removed, combined as `(left operator right)`, and pushed back.

For `ab+c*`, the stack first makes `(a+b)`, then uses it with `c` to get `((a+b)*c)`.

```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    string postToInfix(string postExp) {
        stack<string> expressions;

        for (char token : postExp) {
            if (isspace(static_cast<unsigned char>(token))) {
                continue;
            }

            if (isalnum(static_cast<unsigned char>(token))) {
                expressions.push(string(1, token));
            } else {
                string right = expressions.top();
                expressions.pop();
                string left = expressions.top();
                expressions.pop();

                expressions.push("(" + left + string(1, token) + right + ")");
            }
        }

        return expressions.empty() ? "" : expressions.top();
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(n)`, where `n` is the expression length.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the stack and the created strings.

A postfix operator always uses the last two saved expressions. The expression removed first is the right side, and the next one is the left side. Keeping those two variables separate avoids the common mistake of reversing subtraction or division.

This code skips spaces too, so both `ab+c*` and `a b + c *` are accepted. The input expression is not changed.

```java
import java.util.*;

class Solution {
    public String postToInfix(String postExp) {
        Deque<String> stack = new ArrayDeque<>();

        for (int i = 0; i < postExp.length(); i++) {
            char token = postExp.charAt(i);

            if (Character.isWhitespace(token)) {
                continue;
            }

            if (Character.isLetterOrDigit(token)) {
                stack.push(String.valueOf(token));
            } else {
                String right = stack.pop();
                String left = stack.pop();
                StringBuilder current = new StringBuilder();
                current.append('(')
                       .append(left)
                       .append(token)
                       .append(right)
                       .append(')');
                stack.push(current.toString());
            }
        }

        return stack.isEmpty() ? "" : stack.pop();
    }
}
```

For a single operand such as `a`, no operator is processed and the same operand is returned. A valid postfix input is expected to have enough operands whenever an operator appears.

Time complexity: `O(n)`.
Space complexity: `O(n)`, including intermediate expression strings.

This version reads the postfix expression from right to left. When it sees an operator, it recursively builds the right expression first, then the left expression. That order is important because postfix stores the left operand before the right operand.

The parentheses are added during the return from each recursive call, so operator grouping stays clear.

```java
import java.util.*;

class Solution {
    private int index;

    public String postToInfix(String postExp) {
        index = postExp.length() - 1;
        return build(postExp);
    }

    private String build(String postExp) {
        while (index >= 0 && Character.isWhitespace(postExp.charAt(index))) {
            index--;
        }

        if (index < 0) {
            return "";
        }

        char token = postExp.charAt(index--);

        if (Character.isLetterOrDigit(token)) {
            return String.valueOf(token);
        }

        String right = build(postExp);
        String left = build(postExp);
        return "(" + left + token + right + ")";
    }
}
```

An empty input returns an empty string. The recursive depth can become `O(n)` for a long expression, so this is less suitable if the platform allows very deep inputs.

Time complexity: `O(n)`.
Space complexity: `O(n)` for the recursion stack and returned expression strings.
